    },
    "q-59453": {
      "dom_id": "step-q59453",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14265567/assets/caab152ad60f4096bf701b69bbada8548b6fe3b18e0d9c18bcd6303ba022631f.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of a car. At what times is the car traveling at a constant speed?",
      "worked_solution": "The car is traveling at a constant speed when its acceleration is zero. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is zero.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is zero from $t=20\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=50\\,\\text{s},$ because the graph is horizontal there. The car is traveling at a constant speed in this time interval.",
