    "q-59754": {
      "dom_id": "step-q59754",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A car accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $10$ seconds of its motion. Then, the car decelerates with a constant deceleration of $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ until it stops. Which graph represents the described motion?",
      "worked_solution": "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the car is starting from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $6.$ \n\nHowever, because the car is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10.$ At this time, the car's speed has increased by $6\\cdot 10=60\\text{ m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,60).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14265567/assets/894c969e907020b7a1c4aa0a1296086e516939d0777184fadcb2b474d4ed51da.png)\n\nAfter $t=10$, the car has a deceleration of $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the second line segment is $-3.$ The second line segment stops when the car's speed returns to $0.$ To figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the second line segment.\n\nSince the second line segment passes through the point $(10,60)$ and has a slope of $-3,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-60 & =-3(t-10) \\\\ v-60 & =-3t+30 \\\\ v & =-3t+90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the car is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-3t+90 \\\\ 0 & =-3t+90 \\\\ 3t & =90 \\\\ t & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(30,0).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14265567/assets/484c149bb84666461becbd9961d3631927d4a1f19a58ec116a6e2e047c5432ad.png)",
