    "q-108564": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108564",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "While hunting, a lioness accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $4$ seconds of her motion. Then, the lioness decelerates with a constant deceleration of $4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ until she stops. Which graph represents the described motion?",
      "worked_solution": "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the lioness is starting from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $5.$ \n\nHowever, because the lioness is only accelerating for the first $4$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=4.$ At this time, the lioness' speed has increased by $5\\cdot 4=20\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(4,20).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14265567/assets/62780dd4c7ae5f8a3b674dd8fef84e16d7483b46bea675f078f8169947dd55f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=4,$ the lioness has a deceleration of $4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the second line segment is $-4.$ The second line segment stops when the lioness' speed returns to $0.$ To figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the second line segment.\n\nSince the second line segment passes through the point $(4,20)$ and has a slope of $-4,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-20 & =-4(t-4) \\\\ v-20 & =-4t+16 \\\\ v & =-4t+36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the lioness is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-4t+36 \\\\ 0 & =-4t+36 \\\\ 4t & =36 \\\\ t & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(9,0).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14265567/assets/bbc955f0f759fc845eeba46808904b0f80a57d598eacdbee3f9dd973a74939f6.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
